  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nBehind an overnight frontal passage, dry air and seasonable\ntemperatures will encompass the region through late week. Surface\nanalysis indicates broad high pressure over the interior central\nUS, with the primary cold front pushing through the Appalachians\nand a secondary boundary near the Gulf Coast. A tight pressure\ngradient between the surface high and the departing front will\nmaintain moderate northeasterly winds across the Mid-South this\nafternoon, with gusts reaching up to 25 mph. Diurnal heating\ncombined with post-frontal dry air advection will boost highs\ninto the 80s while driving afternoon relative humidity (RH) down\nsignificantly. Critical RH values below 30 percent are\nanticipated across north Mississippi this afternoon, coinciding\nwith 20-foot winds of 10 to 15 mph. While 10-hour fuel moisture\ncurrently remains above the 10 percent critical threshold, fine\nfuel drying will accelerate today under the influence of gusty\nwinds, low RH, and strong solar radiation.\n\nBenign weather will persist through late week as the Mid-South\nremains in a quasi-zonal upper-level flow pattern, with surface\nhigh pressure dominating the regional weather. Temperatures will\ncontinue to rise a degree or two each day as lower-level warm air\nadvection takes hold, bringing the entire region well into the\nmid-to-upper 80s by late week. With low surface dewpoints\nmaintaining light atmospheric moisture, clear skies will support\nstrong radiational cooling overnight-leading to cooler-than-\nnormal morning low temperatures-and efficient solar heating\nduring the day, yielding warmer-than-normal high temperatures.\nThere is medium confidence in elevated fire weather danger\ndeveloping by late week as 10-hour fuel moisture decreases\nfurther alongside low afternoon Relative Humidity and breezy 20-\nfoot winds.\n\nThe weekend outlook hinges on the evolution and trajectory of a\nsurface low moving across the Gulf of Mexico. Model guidance\ndisplays noticeable spread: the GFS remains more aggressive,\ndeveloping a tropical depression that tracks northward into the\nLower Mississippi Valley, whereas the ECMWF depicts a weaker,\nopen wave progressing across the region. Regardless of exact low\nintensity, an influx of moisture and unsettled weather is\nexpected to return by the weekend, effectively suppressing fire\nweather concerns.",
